"{\"conversationId\":null,\"phase\":\"home\",\"authenticated\":false,\"temporaryChat\":false,\"sidebarOpen\":true,\"navigationPending\":false,\"draft\":{\"text\":\"Extract atomic subject–relation–object claims only from the supplied passage. Preserve explicit negation, aliases, comparison versus identity, and source scope. Attach the printed page and supporting text to each claim. Mark missing or ambiguous information explicitly. Do not add facts from outside the passage.\\n\\nPrinted page: 73\\nSource publication year: 2002\\n\\nPassage:\\nThe Wau Kikik represents the simplest design of all traditional Malay wau. It consists of three different types. The first being, Wau Kikik with a head and dented/concaved. The second, Wau Kikik which is rectangular in shape with a tail and the third, Wau Kikik with a perfect square shape. The Wau Kikik shares a resemblance with the ‘Eddy Kite’ which is also known as the ‘Malay Kite’ by the westerners. Another name for it is Wau Kaca, due to the powdered glass glazed on its string. This is meant to defeat the opponent’s wau once it comes in contact with Wau Kaca during a competition. Today, Wau Kikik is the official Wau for the state of Melaka.\\n\\nIn comparison with other traditional wau, Wau Kikik neither has a hummer nor any ‘sobek’ carvings. With a few basic layers of wau papers, the design of Wau Kikik is without wings like the shape of a leaf which is found in most traditional Malay wau.\\n\",\"revision\":2},\"composer\":{\"action\":\"send\",\"steering\":{\"available\":false,\"pending\":false},\"canSubmit\":true,\"disabled\":false,\"gated\":false,\"offline\":false,\"pendingSubmit\":false,\"uploading\":false},\"model\":{\"thinkingModel\":\"\",\"thinkingEffort\":\"\",\"thinkingHint\":\"\"},\"attachments\":[],\"messageCount\":0,\"messages\":[],\"operation\":null}"